WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this saturday marks 20 years since the  september 11th terrorist attacks 10  years ago a book was published featuring  the letters that family members wrote to  loved ones who were lost on that  terrible terrible day it is called the  legacy letters we met up with three  young people whose letters to their  fathers were included in that book and  they reflected on the past 20 years and  shared their stories of resilience  olivia you were ten  on you were four lauren you were eight  um on 9 11.  i just want you each briefly to tell us  what you remember about that day and the  days following olivia you start please  um i can walk you step by step through  the 11th  it was one of the first few days in  middle school for me olivia perez's  father anthony perez was working as a  technical specialist on the 103rd floor  of the north tower on september 11th  kids were being pulled left and right to  go home early it's fifth period and i'm  in orchestra  and um  they wound up pulling a kid and come on  miss tell us please tell us and just i  don't know i'm not supposed to but okay  you know the world trade center had been  attacked and it was just an instinctual  i mean the sinking feeling in my stomach  you knew your dad was there yeah and i  knew he was no longer here lauren what  do you remember  i have almost the opposite experience if  she can walk you step through step  through her day i've got  five minutes  lauren charette's father mark charrette  usually worked in his office in new  jersey but on september 11th he was  holding an annual planning meeting on  the 100th floor of the north tower  my mother knew he got into the city that  day she sat me and my the older of my  two brothers down and told us that the  towers have been hit  i know i asked her something i couldn't  tell you what it was  [Music]  and then i know several days later and  this conversation i don't really  remember very well that she must have  sat us down a couple days later it was  definitely the weekend and told us he  was dead the only memory i have in  between about it is somewhat recess when  we still weren't sure because i know  somebody was saying something and i know  i got super upset  on you were four  yeah so my account of the at that very  day and the days that follow is  it could be even more vague and hazy  um but i do recall that my father tried  to wave at me yelling my name telling me  to wave through the window of the school  bus  which was something that he never would  never really recall he did otherwise ann  newen's father ken nguyen who lived  through the vietnam war worked in the  navy command center at the pentagon  i noticed that my father didn't come  home i honestly thought that he would  come back at some point and it was a  couple weeks later when the funeral came  when i saw my father's casket was pushed  into the cremation chamber that's when i  knew that  he's not going to come back  it took  even a couple more years after that even  to thoroughly understand  that he's not going to come back and try  to live my life  and develop my identity  without  my father  on lauren and olivia mark the 10th  anniversary of 9 11 by writing legacy  letters to their fathers  dear dad you're the greatest man i could  ever know i'm sure any girl who loves  her father as much as i do would say the  same  but from the short time i got to spend  with you  i know you were the greatest person  alive  now that another 10 years have passed  they look back at those letters  reflected on what they mean to them  today  and what did you want to say  in that letter  you know i don't even  it was cathartic i want him to be proud  i want him  to know that  though his life was cut short that he is  leaving a lasting impact  on the planet  he was  my everything you know  my favorite photo of us  is the one taken on my third birthday  you let me sit  on your shoulders  and we laughed and looked so happy  i am saddened  and sometimes angry  that i was forced to grow up  without  a father  i had learned about terrorism  and the war on terror  a lot of people will not understand what  the word terrorism  means until  you're  directly impacted by that  lauren um what struck me about your  letter it was a series of questions  and i loved your questions because it  was something that we could all relate  to i'm graduating in june will you clap  for me do you think i've made good  friends  i stopped going to church doesn't matter  when you get this will you write back  do you miss me as much as i miss you  will you be at my wedding  do you think i'm picking the right  career  do you still love me  most of all i wanted to ask am i still  your little girl  still your little girl  that question really got to me because i  think every daughter at some point asked  that question of her father it was a  haunting question to me because you  can't get the answers to any of the  questions but they're all questions that  we want to know the answers to  so i shuffled it i think a couple times  but that one was always last it was  always last that was the place to end it  that was the one i wanted the answer to  the most  on you said you promised in your letter  to your dad  that you would make him proud when you  became a man do you think you've done  that yeah ten years later  now i'm about to turn 24.  i feel like i did  grad school is about to be done about to  be finishing up in december  and  that degree was an honor from my father  because he didn't get the chance to  complete his  you all talked really about making your  dads proud  olivia i honor my father every day by  doing something stupid to make someone  smile  and that was anthony perez in a nutshell  i go into work every day with that  because at the end of the day my father  went to work  and he never left i'm still questioning  it i still don't have the answer um if  you've made your dad proud you don't  have the answer yeah no  why not lauren  i don't know every time i ask it of  myself i'm like well what am i doing  with my life now it's like  i like your answer i think i definitely  make like people's lives better so i'd  like to think he'd be proud of that but  i don't have the answer lauren the fact  that you're still here and you're  carrying on your dad's legacy  is really more than enough sitting here  and listening to these these two  beautiful stories i mean my heart is  just breaking yeah and i just want to  give them the biggest hugs right now so  i definitely find a lot of comfort  um being with them  i love talking to them it was comforting  to me to talk to them as well you know  they said the hardest thing about  september 11th is august the month of  august because they know it's coming the  run up to august the interviews from the  media start coming in people start  saying how are you how are you doing and  sometimes they said we want to  acknowledge it other times we don't want  to do anything neither lauren or olivia  have ever seen any footage of 9 11.  they've never looked at it gail it's so  powerful i i have no words no i don't  know well done

